
---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Odds & Pity"
  description="Learn how reward chances work, how progressive odds increase, and how pity protects players from long unlucky streaks."
>
  <section class="intro">
    <span class="eyebrow">LOOT TABLE CONFIGURATION</span>
    <h1>Odds & Pity</h1>
    <p>
      Control how often players receive rewards from containers. Loot Containers
      supports fixed reward odds and progressive odds, allowing server owners to
      choose between consistent probabilities and an increasing chance after
      unsuccessful attempts.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Understanding reward chances</h2>
    <p>
      Every loot table has a reward chance system. When a reward is generated,
      the mod selects an item, builds the item, and then rolls the configured
      reward chance.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the chance roll fails, the generated item is discarded and no reward
      is returned. If the roll succeeds, the selected item is returned to the
      reward system.
    </p>

    <div class="info-card">
      <strong>Important</strong>
      <p>
        Item selection and reward chance are separate operations. Your rarity
        weights determine which item is selected, while the odds system
        determines whether that selected item is awarded.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Fixed odds</h2>
    <p>
      Fixed odds use the same probability for every attempt. Previous failures
      do not increase the chance of receiving a reward.
    </p>

    <h3>Configuration</h3>
    <p>
      Set <code>chanceSystem</code> to <code>FIXED</code> to use fixed odds.
      You can configure the probability using either a percentage or a ratio.
    </p>

    <div class="code-card">
      <div class="code-heading">Percentage example</div>
      <pre><code>{`{
        "chanceSystem": "FIXED",
        "fixedOdds": {
          "format": "PERCENTAGE",
          "percentage": 5.0
        }
      }`}</code></pre>
    </div>

    <div class="code-card">
      <div class="code-heading">Ratio example</div>
      <pre><code>{`{
        "chanceSystem": "FIXED",
        "fixedOdds": {
          "format": "RATIO",
          "numerator": 1,
          "denominator": 100
        }
      }`}</code></pre>
    </div>

    <h3>Percentage format</h3>
    <p>
      The <code>percentage</code> setting represents a percentage from
      0 to 100. For example, <code>5.0</code> means a 5% reward chance.
    </p>

    <h3>Ratio format</h3>
    <p>
      The <code>numerator</code> and <code>denominator</code> settings
      represent a fraction. For example, 1 divided by 100 produces a 1%
      reward chance.
    </p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Configuration</th><th>Reward chance</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1 / 100</td><td>1%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1 / 20</td><td>5%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1 / 10</td><td>10%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1 / 4</td><td>25%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1 / 2</td><td>50%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1 / 1</td><td>100%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="info-card">
      <strong>Validation</strong>
      <p>
        Percentage values are clamped between 0 and 100. Ratio values must
        have a positive numerator and denominator, and the numerator cannot
        exceed the denominator. Invalid values produce a 0% chance.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Progressive odds</h2>
    <p>
      Progressive odds increase the reward chance after unsuccessful attempts.
      This system is commonly known as pity or a pity system.
    </p>
    <p>
      Instead of keeping the same probability forever, the chance gradually
      increases until it reaches the configured maximum.
    </p>

    <div class="code-card">
      <div class="code-heading">Progressive configuration</div>
      <pre><code>{`{
        "chanceSystem": "PROGRESSIVE",
        "progressiveOdds": {
          "startingChance": 1.0,
          "increasePerFailure": 1.0,
          "maximumChance": 100.0,
          "failuresBeforeIncrease": 0,
          "resetAfterReward": true
        }
      }`}</code></pre>
    </div>

    <h3>Progressive settings</h3>

    <div class="setting">
      <h4><code>startingChance</code></h4>
      <span class="default">Default: 1.0</span>
      <p>
        The initial reward chance, expressed as a percentage. A value of
        1.0 means 1%.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="setting">
      <h4><code>increasePerFailure</code></h4>
      <span class="default">Default: 1.0</span>
      <p>
        The number of percentage points added after each eligible failed
        attempt. A value of 1.0 increases the chance by one percentage point.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="setting">
      <h4><code>maximumChance</code></h4>
      <span class="default">Default: 100.0</span>
      <p>
        The highest reward chance the progressive system can reach. The value
        is limited to the range of 0 to 100.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="setting">
      <h4><code>failuresBeforeIncrease</code></h4>
      <span class="default">Default: 0</span>
      <p>
        The number of failures allowed before the progressive increase begins.
        This delays the first increase.
      </p>
      <p>
        For example, setting this to 3 means the first three failures do not
        increase the chance. The fourth attempt uses the starting chance plus
        one increase.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="setting">
      <h4><code>resetAfterReward</code></h4>
      <span class="default">Default: true</span>
      <p>
        Determines whether the player's failure count resets after successfully
        receiving a reward.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>true:</strong> Resets the failure count to zero.</li>
        <li><strong>false:</strong> Keeps the existing failure count.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How progressive chance is calculated</h2>
    <p>
      The progressive system uses the following calculation:
    </p>

    <div class="formula">
      Chance = Starting Chance + (Eligible Failures × Increase Per Failure)
    </div>

    <p>
      The result is then limited by the configured maximum chance.
    </p>

    <p>
      Eligible failures are calculated by subtracting
      <code>failuresBeforeIncrease</code> from the current failure count.
      Negative results are treated as zero.
    </p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>
    <p>
      Imagine a loot table configured with:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Starting chance: 1%</li>
      <li>Increase per failure: 2 percentage points</li>
      <li>Maximum chance: 15%</li>
      <li>Failures before increase: 2</li>
    </ul>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Previous failures</th><th>Next attempt chance</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>0</td><td>1%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>1%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td>1%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3</td><td>3%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4</td><td>5%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5</td><td>7%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6</td><td>9%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>7</td><td>11%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>8</td><td>13%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>9</td><td>15%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>10+</td><td>15%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="info-card">
      <strong>Remember</strong>
      <p>
        The chance is calculated before each attempt. A failed attempt
        increases the failure count for the next attempt, not the attempt
        that just failed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How pity is tracked</h2>
    <p>
      Failure counts are tracked separately for each player and loot table.
      This means progress on one loot table does not affect another.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Each player has their own failure counts.</li>
      <li>Each loot table has an independent failure count per player.</li>
      <li>Failure counts are saved using Minecraft's SavedData system.</li>
      <li>Saved pity data persists across server restarts.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The mod stores this information using the
      <code>lootcontainers_pity</code> saved-data identifier.
    </p>

    <h3>Example</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Player</th><th>Loot table</th><th>Failures</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Player A</td><td>Overworld</td><td>8</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Player A</td><td>Nether</td><td>2</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Player B</td><td>Overworld</td><td>4</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      These are three independent counters. Player A's Overworld failures
      do not affect Player A's Nether progress or Player B's progress.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Recommended starting values</h2>
    <p>
      The following are suggested balancing configurations, not built-in
      defaults. Adjust them to match your server's progression and reward
      frequency.
    </p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Style</th><th>Start</th><th>Increase</th><th>Maximum</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Very rare rewards</td><td>1%</td><td>0.5%</td><td>10%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Balanced rewards</td><td>2%</td><td>1%</td><td>25%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Generous rewards</td><td>5%</td><td>2%</td><td>50%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Frequent rewards</td><td>10%</td><td>5%</td><td>100%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Balancing advice</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Use a lower starting chance for powerful or extremely rare rewards.
      </li>
      <li>
        Increase the chance slowly if containers are opened frequently.
      </li>
      <li>
        Use a lower maximum chance when rewards should remain uncommon.
      </li>
      <li>
        Increase the maximum chance if players should eventually have a much
        better opportunity to receive a reward.
      </li>
      <li>
        Enable resetting if you want each successful reward to begin a new
        pity cycle.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Reward chance field</h2>
    <div class="warning-card">
      <strong>Not implemented yet</strong>
      <p>
        Loot table definitions currently contain a
        <code>rewardChance</code> field with a default value of
        <code>0.25</code>. However, the current reward-generation code does
        not use this field.
      </p>
      <p>
        Fixed and progressive odds currently use their own settings instead.
        The shared reward chance system is planned for a future implementation.
        Changing <code>rewardChance</code> currently has no effect on reward rolls.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

    <details>
      <summary>Does pity guarantee a reward?</summary>
      <p>
        Only if the progressive maximum chance reaches 100%. A lower maximum
        chance does not guarantee a reward, even after many failed attempts.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Does fixed odds use pity?</summary>
      <p>
        No. Fixed odds do not track or increase chances based on failed attempts.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Does pity carry over between loot tables?</summary>
      <p>
        No. Each player has a separate failure counter for every loot table.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>What happens when a player receives a reward?</summary>
      <p>
        If progressive odds are enabled and
        <code>resetAfterReward</code> is true, that player's failure count
        for the current loot table resets to zero.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Are pity counts saved when the server closes?</summary>
      <p>
        Yes. Pity data is stored using Minecraft's SavedData system.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section class="next-page">
    <span class="eyebrow">CONTINUE READING</span>
    <h2>Explore loot tables</h2>
    <p>
      Learn how item pools, rarity weights, reward quantities, and container
      assignments work together.
    </p>
    <a href={`${base}/docs/loot-tables/`}>Read Loot Tables →</a>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .intro {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    color: #b9a4ff;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0.6rem 0 1rem;
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
    letter-spacing: -0.055em;
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 2.7rem 0 1rem;
    font-size: 1.65rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.035em;
  }

  h3 {
    margin: 1.8rem 0 0.7rem;
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }

  h4 {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }

  p, li {
    color: #c3c1d3;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  ul {
    padding-left: 1.4rem;
  }

  li {
    margin-bottom: 0.45rem;
  }

  code {
    color: #e4c7ff;
    background: rgba(190, 150, 255, 0.12);
    padding: 0.15rem 0.35rem;
    border-radius: 5px;
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }

  .info-card,
  .warning-card,
  .code-card,
  .setting,
  .formula,
  .next-page {
    margin: 1.2rem 0;
    padding: 1.25rem;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
    border-radius: 16px;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.035);
  }

  .info-card strong {
    color: #a9dfff;
  }

  .warning-card {
    border-color: rgba(255, 180, 120, 0.25);
  }

  .warning-card strong {
    color: #ffc18d;
  }

  .info-card p,
  .warning-card p {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .code-card {
    padding: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .code-heading {
    padding: 0.8rem 1rem;
    color: #c8b6ff;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
  }

  pre {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 1rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  pre code {
    padding: 0;
    background: none;
    color: #ded9ed;
  }

  .setting {
    margin: 0.8rem 0;
  }

  .setting p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .default {
    display: inline-block;
    margin: 0.25rem 0 0.6rem;
    color: #b9a4ff;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .formula {
    text-align: center;
    color: #d8c7ff;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 1rem 0;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  th, td {
    padding: 0.85rem 0.7rem;
    text-align: left;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
  }

  th {
    color: #d8c7ff;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  td {
    color: #c3c1d3;
  }

  details {
    padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
    margin: 0.7rem 0;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
    border-radius: 12px;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.025);
  }

  summary {
    cursor: pointer;
    color: #e5ddf5;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  details p {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .next-page {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    background: linear-gradient(
      135deg,
      rgba(139, 220, 255, 0.08),
      rgba(243, 166, 232, 0.08)
    );
  }

  .next-page h2 {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
  }

  .next-page a {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    color: #cbb4ff;
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .next-page a:hover {
    color: #ffffff;
  }

  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    table {
      font-size: 0.78rem;
    }

    th, td {
      padding: 0.65rem 0.45rem;
    }
  }
</style>
